WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.490
Have you ever read a fact somewhere
and just… not believed it?

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.640
This is Newlyn in Cornwall,
in the far south-west of England.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.170
When the British government's maps
say that a mountain peak

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.210
is so many metres above sea level,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.360
they are talking about this sea level,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.610
the average sea level here.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.360
There's a little hut on that harbour wall

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.800
and underneath it, there is
a narrow, vertical well

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.820
that connects to the ocean.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.700
So the height of the water in that well
is the height of the tide.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.250
The waves don't make a difference.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.087
So for six years, 1915 to 1921,

00:00:29.087 --> 00:00:32.151
automatic equipment and
human tidal observers

00:00:32.151 --> 00:00:35.510
measured the sea level here, and the
average of all those measurements

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.210
became the Newlyn Datum,

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.920
the zero point from which all heights
in Britain are measured.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.530
The land here is solid granite
stable into deep time,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.840
so it seemed like a reasonable
place to use as a baseline.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.750
So that's fine. That's all sensible.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.470
The fact that I didn't believe is:
this whole part of Britain,

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.520
the whole Cornish peninsula
for hundreds of miles,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:00.810
rises up and falls down by several
centimetres, twice a day, measurably,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.460
because of the weight of the tides.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.230
Now if that was true, it'd be
common knowledge, surely?

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.820
And wouldn't it cause
earthquakes every day?

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.230
I read that and honestly,
I didn't believe it,

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.210
even as I looked round

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.020
and started to find
peer-reviewed papers about it.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.420
So I arranged to talk to one of
the authors of one of those papers,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.420
a world expert on
ocean tide loading and... yeah.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.920
- On some days, Cornwall might
only move up and down by

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.410
three or four centimetres compared
to its average position.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.993
Whereas on other days the movement
might be seven or eight centimetres.

00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:37.470
So a total range of maybe
even 15 centimetres.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.380
There's a horizontal movement
that's about a third or so,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.460
typically, of the vertical
movement in most places.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.710
So how we've measured
the movement of Cornwall

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:50.350
and other places, is using GPS,
the global positioning system.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.020
And we can measure that
distance very precisely indeed

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:58.240
at something like the two or three
millimetre level of precision.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.930
Now there are a lot of errors
that cause a bias in this measurement

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.800
that we have to deal with,
to deal with the effect of, for example,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.120
the change in speed of the radio signal,

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.120
as it goes through different layers
in the Earth's atmosphere, and so on.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.200
But what we can do is we can make
huge numbers of measurements of this.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.884
Our measurement of the average
tidal motion becomes

00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:21.290
very, very accurate and precise indeed.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.530
So we measure that tidal signal,
not just over one tidal cycle,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.130
but actually over a period
of three or four years,

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.370
maybe even as much as 10 or 12 years.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.410
- It makes sense, I guess,
that the weight of water

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.270
would compress what's underneath it.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.553
A few back-of-the-envelope
calculations and you can work out

00:02:38.553 --> 00:02:41.230
that there are gigatons
of water moving out there,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:42.920
in and out twice a day.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.210
And yeah, at the most extreme
high and low tide 

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:50.480
this whole area can move as much as six
or seven centimetres up and down.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.290
But why does it happen here
and not everywhere else?

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.940
- The gravitational forces
of the sun and the moon

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.550
that are what's causing the ocean
to move, and the tides,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:05.970
just happened to synchronise more
or less in their timescale

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:09.840
with the way in which the
North Atlantic is able to move.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.460
It's just a little bit like
if you have a glass of juice,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.550
but if you swirl it around
at just the right speed,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.800
you can build up quite a
big slosh around the edges

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:20.650
of the glass.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.270
Whereas if you swirl it at completely
the wrong speed, as it were, for that glass,

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.130
then you get a much
smaller amount of motion.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.030
Around the coastlines of the major oceans

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.300
are the places where the tidal
loading is generally larger.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.020
So around Western Europe, Greenland,

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.110
and through to the Bay of Fundy
around the Atlantic coast there

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.360
around the Gulf of Alaska
and also around New Zealand.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.890
So the reason it doesn't start causing big things
like earthquakes is because these movements,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:54.320
although they add up to something
appreciable and measurable

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.758
by our new modern, accurate techniques
are actually very small

00:03:59.758 --> 00:04:02.150
relative to the scale of the whole earth.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.010
But that's sufficient for the
most accurate engineering work

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.260
to need to worry about this problem.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.520
- Thanks so much to Professor Clarke,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.820
I've put links to him and to the
papers that I read in the description.

